From 2adfbb88fbac6269a17ea8d4083f9b1e9d471033 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ebg-ant <296364998+ebg-ant@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 07:07:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] ci: the lines of `make lint` run side by side in the gate
 and in CI: one minute instead of two

`make lint` is two dozen checks that know nothing of one another, and make runs them one after another: 16 s at the start of
the night, 141 s now, on one core of four. It is the first step of every `make gate`, and all that CI runs for a draft.

scripts/side-by-side.py reads the rule off the Makefile and runs its lines as many at a time as there are cores: 67 s, as
long as the longest line and no longer. Every line runs whatever became of the others, what each printed is shown whole and in the
order of the rule, and the lines that failed are named. What only make can work out it refuses.

The rule stays where it is and as it is: whoever adds a check adds a line to it, and `make lint` means what it meant.
